START A CLUB

Bring OCR to Your Community

The Obstacle Course Racing Association of Ireland (OCRA ÉIREANN) wants to support people interested in creating new obstacle racing clubs and training communities across Ireland.

A strong national sport is built through active local communities.

If there is no OCR club in your area, starting one may be easier than you think.


You Don't Need a Full Obstacle Facility

A new club does not need to begin with a permanent obstacle course.

OCR training can combine:

- Running
- Strength and conditioning
- Grip training
- Carries
- Climbing
- Trail running
- Bodyweight training
- Visits to existing OCR facilities
- Sessions using suitable obstacle equipment

The important first step is often simply bringing together people who want to train.


Build a Local Community

A club can provide:

- Regular training
- Training partners
- Support for beginners
- Race preparation
- Social activities
- Competitive development
- Coaching opportunities
- Youth development
- Connections with other OCR clubs
- A stronger local presence for the sport


Getting Started

If you are considering starting a club, useful first steps include:

- Finding other interested people
- Identifying suitable training locations
- Establishing regular sessions
- Agreeing how the club will operate
- Considering safety and insurance
- Identifying coaching requirements
- Establishing safeguarding arrangements where required
- Connecting with OCRA


OCRA Support

OCRA wants to help new clubs become part of the wider Irish obstacle racing community.

This can include connections with:

- Existing OCR clubs
- Coaches
- Training facilities
- Events
- Technical Officials
- Club development resources
- National programmes


Club Affiliation

Once established, clubs can also explore formal affiliation with OCRA.

Affiliation provides a recognised connection between the club and the national organisation for obstacle sport.


